    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d52">

                <pb facs="0051" n="39" sameAs="frus:frus1941v01/pg39"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.0011 European War 1939/12290: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in Finland</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Schoenfeld</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Helsinki</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1941-06-22T11:00:00+02:00">June 22, 1941—11
                            a.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received June 22—10:25 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>211. Finnish General Staff informs our Military Attaché this morning that German
                    statement early today regarding alleged Finnish support of German attack on
                    Soviet Union signifies that neither Finns nor Germans will take offensive
                    against Russians on Finnish frontier and that Finnish support of Germans will be
                    defensive. General Staff denied that there were any German troops in southern
                    Finland as reported in my telegram 210, June 21, 2 p.m.<anchor n="97"
                        xml:id="d52-f1"/> Huthsteiner was told that, according to information
                    received by Finnish General Staff, Third Russian Army Corps was destroyed this
                    morning in the Grodno area of Poland. General Staff is moving to field
                    headquarters. It is not intended to blackout Helsinki for the present and the
                    civil population remains calm.</p>
                <p>While aboard a sailboat yesterday afternoon in Helsinki Bay, I personally saw two
                    German E–boats enter the harbor.</p>
                <p>Please advise War Department.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Schoenfeld</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
